#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 1140.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167781} :activity/id #uuid "b36ed83a-cc37-5ff2-a68e-2a0e28c0b24e" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048270 :topic/id #uuid "8e43115f-4416-5ec0-b698-3e53a959567b" :topic/math-academy-id 3286} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167713 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166847 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166697 :question/id #uuid "699d9c0c-bb98-5710-8d98-10b6f35ab202" :question/math-academy-id "e-8911"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e9d77603-c824-5160-80fc-3d10353fe243" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048412} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049605}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167115} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167116} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487306} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487321} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487345} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487380} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524964} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524987} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549984}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Conditional Probability Using Bayes' Theorem"} :step/id #uuid "7e6025e7-7c05-542d-a78a-f9d7e627be29" :step/math-academy-id 33287 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167772}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167714 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166848 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166698 :question/id #uuid "284f5029-19eb-53d4-a3b7-0f66d33b9cc9" :question/math-academy-id "e-8912"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3a4fee2d-88c3-5381-bd55-98990abc7df3" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167117} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167119} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275200} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487319} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487354} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487355} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487367} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487403} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493912} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493967} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549938}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving for an Unknown Conditional Probability"} :step/id #uuid "fd5da403-f379-5dee-9a50-ecafcf083c36" :step/math-academy-id 33288} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167772 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166877 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166482 :question/id #uuid "bc694d40-d150-55e6-981f-0ca6b5d91e21" :question/math-academy-id "e-10418"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6c467adb-9d0e-5b33-b81e-4959e2703535" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167052} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167118} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487295} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487323} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487326} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487364} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487402} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493903} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524963} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186525053} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186525062} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549926}] :knowledge-point/title "Applying Bayes’ Theorem to a Situation in Context"} :step/id #uuid "7a97606a-98ac-50e6-9e3c-78ae18820b2e" :step/math-academy-id 39256 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167714}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167781 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168143 :tutorial/content "In this lesson, we will generalize Bayes' theorem to deal with situations where the sample space $\\mathcal S$ is partitioned into a disjoint union of multiple events. \n\nSuppose we have three events $A_1,A_2,$ and $A_3$ that form a disjoint union of the sample space $\\mathcal S,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/89/89a78ef526fcd29a59bacd4d6ae1a456a596d6616af7453f1ba530a610d9a2ac.png)\n\n \n\nNow consider an event $B$ that also lies in the sample space:\n\n![](images/da/daa96423e45f9f80897e2c2d61177eab2faff77f440921866d1b81b7ef2cfe09.png)\n\n \n\nBayes' theorem states that\n\n$$\nP(A_1|B)=\\dfrac{P(B|A_1)P(A_1)}{P(B)}.\n$$\n\nNow, the law of total probability tells us that\n\n$$\nP(B)=P(B|A_1)P(A_1) + P(B|A_2)P(A_2) + P(B|A_3)P(A_3).\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into Bayes' theorem, we get\n\n$$\nP(A_1 | B) = \\dfrac{P(B|A_1) P(A_1)}{P(B|A_1)P(A_1) + P(B|A_2)P(A_2) + P(B|A_3)P(A_3)} .\n$$\n\nUsing similar arguments, we can say that for $A_k$ for $k=1,2,3,$ we have\n\n$$\nP(A_k | B) = \\dfrac{P(B|A_k) P(A_k)}{P(B|A_1)P(A_1) + P(B|A_2)P(A_2) + P(B|A_3)P(A_3)}\n$$\n\nwhich is usually written more compactly as\n\n$$\nP(A_k | B) = \\dfrac{P(B|A_k) P(A_k)}{\\sum_{i=1}^3 P(B|A_i)P(A_i)}.\n$$\n\nIt's easy to see how this generalizes. If the disjoint union $A_1 \\sqcup A_2 \\sqcup \\ldots \\sqcup A_n$ covers the entire sample space, Bayes' theorem combined with the law of total probability gives us the following:\n\n$$\nP(A_k | B) = \\dfrac{P(B|A_k) P(A_k)}{\\sum_{i=1}^n P(B|A_i)P(A_i)}\n$$\n\nLet's see an example of how to apply this theorem." :tutorial/id #uuid "bf17685f-9890-54cc-b8a2-15815558e87a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6969 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "b6bd91e8-6598-5c56-9fa4-bf1144fc830d" :step/math-academy-id 42168 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167713}}] :activity/title "Extending Bayes' Theorem" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186167047}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186494026} :activity/id #uuid "cebad3dd-e992-52ed-b284-8c84be80cca2" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048270 :topic/id #uuid "8e43115f-4416-5ec0-b698-3e53a959567b" :topic/math-academy-id 3286} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493998 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167118 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "88acdedb-86f7-5376-9663-ed92e647a982" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.174" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163787} {:answer/id #uuid "b8298e93-12cb-5948-9b0c-4d75b7d3a684" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.199" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163788} {:answer/id #uuid "cca46cae-3f7b-5c75-a82f-2a44e0f21afb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.203" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163789} {:answer/id #uuid "1bae2866-554f-511e-86e4-7f2ca81b63aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163790} {:answer/id #uuid "7352704f-f734-5a12-b655-8b2ef85bf0d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.191" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163791}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163788} :answer-field/id #uuid "42a0e7e2-d14d-5d36-a9fb-b4d391b04b02" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163786}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "dea495bf-07d9-54cd-baea-bb953539d3dd" :question/math-academy-id "q-148673" :question/problem "Arthur and Sara are finding the best recipe for chocolate muffins, and they test three different oven cooking modes, mode $1,$ mode $2,$ and mode $3.$ They also try two types of yeast, A and B.\n\n- They use mode $1$ to cook the $30%$ of their muffins, and $90%$ of those muffins turn out well.\n\n- They use mode $2$ to cook the $40%$ of their muffins, and $85%$ of those muffins turn out well.\n\n- They use mode $3$ to cook the $30%$ of their muffins, and $88%$ of those muffins turn out well.\n\nIf $20%$ of all muffins was prepared with yeast A, and $87%$ of yeast A muffins turn out well, what is the probability that a randomly chosen good muffin is done with yeast A? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "Let $A$ be the event that a muffin is made with yeast A, $G$ be the event that a muffin turns out well, and ${M}_{1},{M}_{2},{M}_{3}$ be the event that a muffin is cooked with mode $1,$ $2,$ or $3,$ respectively. \n\nLet's break down the information given in the problem statement:\n\n- From the bullet points, we have the following information:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & P({M}_{1})=0.3 &  & P(G|{M}_{1})=0.9 \\\\  & P({M}_{2})=0.4 &  & P(G|{M}_{2})=0.85 \\\\  & P({M}_{3})=0.3 &  & P(G|{M}_{3})=0.88\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe disjoint union ${M}_{1}⊔{M}_{2}⊔{M}_{3}$ covers the entire sample space, and we have\n\n$$\nP({M}_{1})+P({M}_{2})+P({M}_{3})=0.3+0.4+0.3=1.\n$$\n\n- Since $20%$ of muffins are made with yeast A, we have\n\n$$\nP(A)=0.2.\n$$\n\n- Since $87%$ of muffins made with yeast A turn out well, we have\n\n$$\nP(G|A)=0.87.\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that a randomly chosen good muffin is made with yeast A, which is represented as $P(A|G).$ \n\nUsing Bayes' theorem combined with the law of total probability, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(A|G) & =\\frac{P(G|A)P(A)}{P(G)} \\\\  & =\\frac{P(G|A)P(A)}{P(G|{M}_{1})P({M}_{1})+P(G|{M}_{2})P({M}_{2})+P(G|{M}_{3})P({M}_{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(0.87)(0.2)}{(0.9)(0.3)+(0.85)(0.4)+(0.88)(0.3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.174}{0.874} \\\\  & ≈0.199,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."} :step/id #uuid "444842eb-2ff5-586c-acce-f30990803c2c" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186494071}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186494026 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167117 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8c5c4998-4883-5ba4-a08f-5c9044a9ad93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.584" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163781} {:answer/id #uuid "a9eec898-008f-5aeb-9d82-da5f4e5795a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.642" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163782} {:answer/id #uuid "c1f06ee9-62a4-5d90-a2a6-5b8a41e3e676" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.527" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163783} {:answer/id #uuid "088444df-24a0-506a-90eb-e82abac77d6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.621" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163784} {:answer/id #uuid "4988a61c-c2b9-57dd-87f0-60ed10f9fa86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.592" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163785}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163785} :answer-field/id #uuid "4141da06-c478-52fb-832c-70aa2c84d531" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163780}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "e39e7e2c-b39e-5302-b92e-8f13f6ffd98c" :question/math-academy-id "q-147235" :question/problem "The students in a particular school are split among three classes, class $1,$ class $2,$ and class $3.$\n\n- Class $1$ contains $25%$ of the students in the school, and $55%$ of those students take biology.\n\n- Class $2$ contains $35%$ of the students in the school, and $40%$ of those students take biology.\n\n- Class $3$ contains $40%$ of the students in the school.\n\nIf $45%$ of students in the entire school are female, $80%$ of female students take biology, and $70%$ of biology students are female, what is the probability that a randomly selected student from class $3$ takes biology? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "Let $F$ be the event that a student is female, $B$ be the event that a student takes biology, and ${C}_{1},{C}_{2},{C}_{3}$ be the event that a student is in class $1,$ $2,$ or $3,$ respectively. \n\nLet's break down the information given in the problem statement:\n\n- From the bullet points, we have the following information:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & P({C}_{1})=0.25 &  & P(B|{C}_{1})=0.55 \\\\  & P({C}_{2})=0.35 &  & P(B|{C}_{2})=0.4 \\\\  & P({C}_{3})=0.4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe disjoint union ${C}_{1}⊔{C}_{2}⊔{C}_{3}$ covers the entire sample space, and we have\n\n$$\nP({C}_{1})+P({C}_{2})+P({C}_{3})=0.25+0.35+0.4=1.\n$$\n\n- Since $45%$ of students in the entire school are female, we have\n\n$$\nP(F)=0.45.\n$$\n\n- Since $80%$ of females take biology, we have\n\n$$\nP(B|F)=0.8.\n$$\n\n- Since $70%$ of biology students are female, we have\n\n$$\nP(F|B)=0.7.\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that a randomly chosen student from class $3$ takes biology, which is represented as $P(B|{C}_{3}).$ \n\nUsing Bayes' theorem combined with the law of total probability, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(F|B) & =\\frac{P(B|F)P(F)}{P(B)} \\\\ P(F|B) & =\\frac{P(B|F)P(F)}{P(B|{C}_{1})P({C}_{1})+P(B|{C}_{2})P({C}_{2})+P(B|{C}_{3})P({C}_{3})} \\\\ 0.7 & =\\frac{(0.8)(0.45)}{(0.55)(0.25)+(0.4)(0.35)+P(B|{C}_{3})(0.4)} \\\\ 0.7 & =\\frac{0.36}{0.2775+0.4P(B|{C}_{3})}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $P(B|{C}_{3}),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.7(0.2775+0.4P(B|{C}_{3})) & =0.36 \\\\ 0.2775+0.4P(B|{C}_{3}) & =\\frac{0.36}{0.7} \\\\ 0.2775+0.4P(B|{C}_{3}) & =\\frac{18}{35} \\\\ 0.4P(B|{C}_{3}) & =\\frac{18}{35}-0.2775 \\\\ P(B|{C}_{3}) & =\\frac{1}{0.4}(\\frac{18}{35}-0.2775) \\\\ P(B|{C}_{3}) & ≈0.592\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places."} :step/id #uuid "84fa05d5-af97-59d8-b33c-dd583737eabb" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186494060}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186494049 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493903 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0efdb1e8-9c52-52d4-b930-051a0d37a2ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.08" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493415} {:answer/id #uuid "0fd36a06-2c0d-5867-b8fb-b56a3f2b9dcd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.369" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493416} {:answer/id #uuid "9664ae4b-b407-5bfc-92af-7d685fbf9717" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.431" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493626} {:answer/id #uuid "bd528e4f-8665-5726-9e95-265f3f2fbb42" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.235" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493702} {:answer/id #uuid "e4fb6fc7-b587-569b-b74c-57c52fc7da96" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.35" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493756}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493626} :answer-field/id #uuid "ae7e24b9-313e-560b-9e0c-e4316aa55761" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493862}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "2ad5265b-a872-53d0-b3f1-65585e111dd6" :question/math-academy-id "q-149494" :question/problem "In a nature park, there are $4$ species of squirrels, each with the same population.\n\n- Among the squirrels of species $1,$ $12%$ are born in captivity.\n\n- Among the squirrels of species $2,$ $5%$ are born in captivity.\n\n- Among the squirrels of species $3,$ $6%$ are born in captivity.\n\n- Among the squirrels of species $4,$ $3%$ are born in captivity.\n\nIf $35%$ of the squirrels are adult females, and $8%$ of the adult female squirrels are born in captivity, what is the probability that a randomly chosen squirrel born in captivity is an adult female? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Let $F$ be the event that a squirrel is an adult female, $C$ be the event that a squirrel is born in captivity, and ${S}_{1},{S}_{2},{S}_{3},{S}_{4}$ be the event that a squirrel is of species $1,$ $2,$ $3,$ or $4,$ respectively. \n\nLet's break down the information given in the problem statement:\n\n- Each species has the same number of squirrels, so we have the following information:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P({S}_{1})=P({S}_{2})=P({S}_{3})=P({S}_{4})=0.25\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- From the bullet points, we have the following information:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & P(C|{S}_{1})=0.12 \\\\  & P(C|{S}_{2})=0.05 \\\\  & P(C|{S}_{3})=0.06 \\\\  & P(C|{S}_{4})=0.03\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe disjoint union ${S}_{1}⊔{S}_{2}⊔{S}_{3}⊔{S}_{4}$ covers the entire sample space, and we have\n\n$$\nP({S}_{1})+P({S}_{2})+P({S}_{3})+P({S}_{4})=0.25+0.25+0.25+0.25=1.\n$$\n\n- Since $35%$ of the squirrels are adult females, we have\n\n$$\nP(F)=0.35.\n$$\n\n- Since $8%$ of adult females are born in captivity, we have\n\n$$\nP(C|F)=0.08.\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that a randomly chosen squirrel born in captivity is an adult female, which is represented as $P(F|C).$ \n\nUsing Bayes' theorem combined with the law of total probability, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(F|C) & =\\frac{P(C|F)P(F)}{P(C)} \\\\  & =\\frac{P(C|F)P(F)}{P(C|{S}_{1})P({S}_{1})+P(C|{S}_{2})P({S}_{2})+P(C|{S}_{3})P({S}_{3})+P(C|{S}_{3})P({S}_{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(0.08)(0.35)}{(0.12)(0.25)+(0.05)(0.25)+(0.06)(0.25)+(0.03)(0.25)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.028}{0.065} \\\\  & ≈0.431,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."} :step/id #uuid "b32c5a3a-fa6d-50eb-94d9-f684a00e861d"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186494060 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493912 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08123d50-e1d2-5e75-87c8-dc4ecec898fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.05" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493402} {:answer/id #uuid "2315c5ef-ec06-536c-af7c-d82e621541c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.03" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493448} {:answer/id #uuid "68e491ab-da9f-5329-9aaa-a11a1bebb3d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.047" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493547} {:answer/id #uuid "75ac382e-a141-5c83-ade6-7dc3a4ffc925" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.035" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493566} {:answer/id #uuid "e67f42bf-f74e-5283-955d-76c3d0573b19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.040" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493760}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493760} :answer-field/id #uuid "f3f4b001-3f5d-563e-a82a-efaf92b09aad" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493885}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "39f657eb-aa97-5394-b0e6-ecba3bf80018" :question/math-academy-id "q-148666" :question/problem "A manufacturing company has three sites, factory $1,$ factory $2,$ and factory $3.$ They all produce two types of light bulbs: A and B.\n\n- Factory $1$ produces $45%$ of the entire production, and $5%$ of those bulbs are defective.\n\n- Factory $2$ produces $45%$ of the entire production.\n\n- Factory $3$ produces $10%$ of the entire production, and $1%$ of those bulbs are defective.\n\nIf $57%$ of all light bulbs are of type A, $3%$ of type A light bulbs are defective, and $41%$ of defective light bulbs are of type A, what is the probability that a randomly chosen light bulb from factory $2$ is defective? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Let $A$ be the event that a light bulb is of type A, $D$ be the event that a light bulb is defective, and ${F}_{1},{F}_{2},{F}_{3}$ be the event that a light bulb is produced in factory $1,$ $2,$ or $3,$ respectively. \n\nLet's break down the information given in the problem statement:\n\n- From the bullet points, we have the following information:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & P({F}_{1})=0.45 &  & P(D|{F}_{1})=0.05 \\\\  & P({F}_{2})=0.45 \\\\  & P({F}_{3})=0.1 &  & P(D|{F}_{3})=0.01\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe disjoint union ${F}_{1}⊔{F}_{2}⊔{F}_{3}$ covers the entire sample space, and we have\n\n$$\nP({F}_{1})+P({F}_{2})+P({F}_{3})=0.45+0.45+0.1=1.\n$$\n\n- Since $57%$ of all light bulbs are of type A, we have\n\n$$\nP(A)=0.57.\n$$\n\n- Since $3%$ of type A light bulbs are defective, we have\n\n$$\nP(D|A)=0.03.\n$$\n\n- Since $41%$ of defective light bulbs are of type A, we have\n\n$$\nP(A|D)=0.41\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that a randomly chosen light bulb from factory $2$ is defective, which is represented as $P(D|{F}_{2}).$ \n\nUsing Bayes' theorem combined with the law of total probability, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(A|D) & =\\frac{P(D|A)P(A)}{P(D)} \\\\ P(A|D) & =\\frac{P(D|A)P(A)}{P(D|{F}_{1})P({F}_{1})+P(D|{F}_{2})P({F}_{2})+P(D|{F}_{3})P({F}_{3})} \\\\ 0.41 & =\\frac{(0.03)(0.57)}{(0.05)(0.45)+P(D|{F}_{2})(0.45)+(0.01)(0.1)} \\\\ 0.41 & =\\frac{0.0171}{0.0235+0.45P(D|{F}_{2})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $P(D|{F}_{2}),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.41(0.0235+0.45P(D|{F}_{2})) & =0.0171 \\\\ 0.0235+0.45P(D|{F}_{2}) & =\\frac{0.0171}{0.41} \\\\ 0.0235+0.45P(D|{F}_{2}) & =\\frac{171}{4100} \\\\ 0.45P(D|{F}_{2}) & =\\frac{171}{4100}-0.0235 \\\\ P(D|{F}_{2}) & =\\frac{1}{0.45}(\\frac{171}{4100}-0.0235) \\\\ P(D|{F}_{2}) & ≈0.040\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places."} :step/id #uuid "e03d6f33-2586-5d57-a7b2-26ad674790fd" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493998}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186494071 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493967 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aad9a2ec-8c38-50c5-a148-e148cf1a201f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.307" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493659} {:answer/id #uuid "cb08ac4c-bcdb-5bf3-803d-48abd3c44b23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.546" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493717} {:answer/id #uuid "db3514aa-7da2-58cd-a765-4883b4c73d8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.135" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493736} {:answer/id #uuid "e44bfd71-59f5-5456-8e29-b331fc4b994f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.398" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493751} {:answer/id #uuid "e5ca70ca-440e-5d09-bd91-1a12c84d2bf9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.252" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493758}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493751} :answer-field/id #uuid "b2b5eda7-fc54-5354-9c43-01751b2546c8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493866}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "deb0627a-b1f4-5ecd-95e2-372ffa642f2c" :question/math-academy-id "q-149493" :question/problem "A volleyball school for children splits the players into three teams, team $1$, team $2,$ and team $3.$ \n\n- $44%$ of children are in team $1,$ and $23%$ of children in this team have been playing for over two years.\n\n- $32%$ of children are in team $2,$ and $26%$ of children in this team have been playing for over two years.\n\n- $24%$ of children are in team $3.$\n\nIf $7%$ of all children can play in the blocker position, $40%$ of children who can play as blockers have been playing for over two years, and $10%$ of children who have been playing for over two years can play as blockers, what is the probability that a randomly chosen child in team $3$ has been playing for over two years? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Let $B$ be the event that a child can play in the blocker position, $I$ be the event that a child has been playing for over two years, and ${T}_{1},{T}_{2},{T}_{3}$ be the events that a child is in team $1,$ $2,$ or $3,$ respectively. \n\nLet's break down the information given in the problem statement:\n\n- From the bullet points, we have the following information:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & P({T}_{1})=0.44 &  & P(I|{T}_{1})=0.23 \\\\  & P({T}_{2})=0.32 &  & P(I|{T}_{2})=0.26 \\\\  & P({T}_{3})=0.24\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe disjoint union ${T}_{1}⊔{T}_{2}⊔{T}_{3}$ covers the entire sample space, and we have\n\n$$\nP({T}_{1})+P({T}_{2})+P({T}_{3})=0.44+0.32+0.24=1.\n$$\n\n- Since $7%$ of children can play in the blocker position, we have\n\n$$\nP(B)=0.07.\n$$\n\n- Since $40%$ of children who can play as blockers have been playing for over two years, we have\n\n$$\nP(I|B)=0.4.\n$$\n\n- Since $10%$ of children who have been playing for over two years can play as blockers, we have\n\n$$\nP(B|I)=0.1.\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that a randomly chosen child in team 3 has been playing for over two years, which is represented as $P(I|{T}_{3}).$\n\nUsing Bayes' theorem combined with the law of total probability, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(B|I) & =\\frac{P(I|B)P(B)}{P(I)} \\\\ P(B|I) & =\\frac{P(I|B)P(B)}{P(I|{T}_{1})P({T}_{1})+P(I|{T}_{2})P({T}_{2})+P(I|{T}_{3})P({T}_{3})} \\\\ 0.1 & =\\frac{(0.4)(0.07)}{(0.23)(0.44)+(0.26)(0.32)+P(I|{T}_{3})(0.24)} \\\\ 0.1 & =\\frac{0.028}{0.1844+0.24P(I|{T}_{3})}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $P(I|{T}_{3}),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.1(0.1844+0.24P(I|{T}_{3})) & =0.028 \\\\ 0.1844+0.24P(I|{T}_{3}) & =\\frac{0.028}{0.1} \\\\ 0.1844+0.24P(I|{T}_{3}) & =\\frac{7}{25} \\\\ 0.24P(I|{T}_{3}) & =\\frac{7}{25}-0.1844 \\\\ P(I|{T}_{3}) & =\\frac{1}{0.24}(\\frac{7}{25}-0.1844) \\\\ P(I|{T}_{3}) & ≈0.398\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places."} :step/id #uuid "f74c9cc2-11d5-584d-b43f-9184a7e00330" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186494049}}] :activity/title "Extending Bayes' Theorem" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493388}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550059} :activity/id #uuid "4a76ab2b-fb4c-5063-aa81-3107b8d38d69" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048270 :topic/id #uuid "8e43115f-4416-5ec0-b698-3e53a959567b" :topic/math-academy-id 3286} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550043 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549938 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "50e9c58b-4fb9-5497-9482-e8f285727015" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.055" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549479} {:answer/id #uuid "5b9c5c15-f6a2-5b4a-a895-22168d05307d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.095" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549499} {:answer/id #uuid "8a26048a-fd81-56ee-8af6-bd08ebb325b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.047" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549578} {:answer/id #uuid "a2ac414e-15b9-5ce9-93e3-a7f2a6d829da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.019" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549625} {:answer/id #uuid "c97eb869-625e-551f-be3b-c0ff2ec27876" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.028" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549690}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549690} :answer-field/id #uuid "2d9e6faf-9e8f-5d9c-aa84-79845b127ed4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549823}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "1787d264-9d95-57d2-8330-7eec4c052cfe" :question/math-academy-id "q-148271" :question/problem "A medical center is investigating the correlation between scoliosis and sports practice in a sample of children.\n\n- $25%$ of children in the sample don't practice sports, and $2.5%$ of those children have scoliosis.\n\n- $30%$ of children in the sample practice one sport.\n\n- $45%$ of children in the sample practice two sports, and $2.8%$ of those children have scoliosis.\n\nIf $48%$ of children in the entire sample are female, $2.6%$ of female children have scoliosis, and $46%$ of children with scoliosis are female, what is the probability that a randomly chosen child who practices one sport has scoliosis? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Let $F$ be the event that a child is female, $C$ be the event that a child has scoliosis, and ${S}_{0},{S}_{1},{S}_{2}$ be the event that a child practices $0,$ $1,$ or $2$ sports, respectively.\n\nLet's break down the information given in the problem statement:\n\n- From the bullet points, we have the following information:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & P({S}_{0})=0.25 &  & P(C∣{S}_{0})=0.025 \\\\  & P({S}_{1})=0.30 \\\\  & P({S}_{2})=0.45 &  & P(C∣{S}_{2})=0.028\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe disjoint union ${S}_{0}⊔{S}_{1}⊔{S}_{2}$ covers the entire sample space, and we have\n\n$$\nP({S}_{0})+P({S}_{1})+P({S}_{2})=0.25+0.30+0.45=1.\n$$\n\n- Since $48%$ of students in the entire sample are female, we have\n\n$$\nP(F)=0.48.\n$$\n\n- Since $2.6%$ of females have scoliosis, we have\n\n$$\nP(C∣F)=0.026.\n$$\n\n- Since $46%$ of children with scoliosis are female, we have\n\n$$\nP(F∣C)=0.46.\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that a randomly chosen child who practices one sport has scoliosis, which is represented as $P(C∣{S}_{1}).$\n\nUsing Bayes' theorem combined with the law of total probability, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(F∣C) & =\\frac{P(C∣F)P(F)}{P(C)} \\\\ P(F∣C) & =\\frac{P(C∣F)P(F)}{P(C∣{S}_{0})P({S}_{0})+P(C∣{S}_{1})P({S}_{1})+P(C∣{S}_{2})P({S}_{2})} \\\\ 0.46 & =\\frac{(0.026)(0.48)}{(0.25)(0.025)+P(C∣{S}_{1})(0.3)+(0.028)(0.45)} \\\\ 0.46 & =\\frac{0.0125}{0.0189+0.3P(C∣{S}_{1})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $P(C∣{S}_{1}),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.46(0.0189+0.3P(C∣{S}_{1})) & =0.0125 \\\\ 0.0189+0.3P(C∣{S}_{1}) & =\\frac{0.0125}{0.46} \\\\ 0.3P(C∣{S}_{1}) & =\\frac{0.0125}{0.46}-0.0189 \\\\ P(C∣{S}_{1}) & =\\frac{1}{0.3}(\\frac{0.0125}{0.46}-0.0189) \\\\ P(C∣{S}_{1}) & ≈0.028\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places."} :step/id #uuid "39ce452f-966f-5775-99e0-20fb8ca0eef9"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550059 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549984 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "161fb25c-96cf-5b02-8d7d-25af5ca8f28a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.365" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549357} {:answer/id #uuid "3abf36e3-3cf2-5339-8e78-9d57d7acb48a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.286" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549435} {:answer/id #uuid "408cef73-e79b-5f84-909b-bb78b0af2709" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.322" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549446} {:answer/id #uuid "c7987182-3703-5340-83e1-52554f33e28f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.444" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549688} {:answer/id #uuid "cb1644a5-6db3-535c-a833-2c384185599d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.403" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549693}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549693} :answer-field/id #uuid "266b7c98-bb0c-5250-98ae-54d840c489c2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549819}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "9c281bbc-e5bb-5c0b-9b81-0eabf770ee2e" :question/math-academy-id "q-147885" :question/problem "The disjoint union ${A}_{1}⊔{A}_{2}⊔{A}_{3}⊔{A}_{4}$ of events ${A}_{1},{A}_{2},{A}_{3},{A}_{4}$ covers the entire sample space. Compute $P({A}_{2}|B)$ given the following probabilities:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & P(B|{A}_{1})=0.25, & P({A}_{1})=0.28 \\\\  & P(B|{A}_{2})=0.3, &  \\\\  & P(B|{A}_{3})=0.28, & P({A}_{3})=0.18 \\\\  & P(B|{A}_{4})=0.1, & P({A}_{4})=0.22\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRound your answer to $3$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "If the disjoint union ${A}_{1}⊔{A}_{2}⊔…⊔{A}_{n}$ covers the entire sample space, Bayes' theorem combined with the law of total probability gives us the following:\n\n$$\nP({A}_{k}|B)=\\frac{P(B|{A}_{k})P({A}_{k})}{P(B)}=\\frac{P(B|{A}_{k})P({A}_{k})}{{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}P(B|{A}_{i})P({A}_{i})}\n$$\n\nwhere $k=1,2,…,n.$\n\nIn our case, $n=4,$ and ${A}_{1}⊔{A}_{2}⊔{A}_{3}⊔{A}_{4}$ covers the entire sample space. Consequently, we have\n\n$$\nP({A}_{1})+P({A}_{2})+P({A}_{3})+P({A}_{4})=1.\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P({A}_{2}) & =1-(P({A}_{1})+P({A}_{3})+P({A}_{4})) \\\\  & =1-(0.28+0.18+0.22) \\\\  & =0.32.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P({A}_{2}|B) & =\\frac{P(B|{A}_{2})P({A}_{2})}{{\\sum }_{i=1}^{4}P(B|{A}_{i})P({A}_{i})} \\\\  & =\\frac{P(B|{A}_{2})P({A}_{2})}{P(B|{A}_{1})P({A}_{1})+P(B|{A}_{2})P({A}_{2})+P(B|{A}_{3})P({A}_{3})+P(B|{A}_{4})P({A}_{4})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(0.3)(0.32)}{(0.25)(0.28)+(0.3)(0.32)+(0.28)(0.18)+(0.1)(0.22)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.096}{0.2384} \\\\  & ≈0.403.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "5af77563-9292-5cfd-aaa2-e0647be6b4b1" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550109}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550081 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549926 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "144c9c56-d288-5656-9e9a-114c97ea9460" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.348" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549350} {:answer/id #uuid "39018054-1073-5cec-87c0-3be506864193" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.415" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549432} {:answer/id #uuid "7aae2ee8-a81d-50e2-98ea-e2df978f83a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.48" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549546} {:answer/id #uuid "7dd4d43d-b30b-5f97-8032-3941bf17d1fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.552" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549552} {:answer/id #uuid "85700ac7-cad1-590c-a143-b418ccc70b5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.52" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549565}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549350} :answer-field/id #uuid "af85ec6e-7016-53c3-802c-9198ac759320" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549885}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "095773d5-faca-5dcc-94ed-85e3db151ccc" :question/math-academy-id "q-148082" :question/problem "A medical center is investigating the correlation between scoliosis and sports practice in a sample of children.\n\n- $25%$ of children in the sample don't practice sports, and $3%$ of those children have scoliosis.\n\n- $30%$ of children in the sample practice one sport, and $2.5%$ of those children have scoliosis.\n\n- $45%$ of children in the sample practice two sports, and $3.3%$ of those children have scoliosis.\n\nIf $52%$ of children in the entire sample are female, and $2%$ of female children have scoliosis, what is the probability that a randomly chosen child with scoliosis is female? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Let $F$ be the event that a child is female, $C$ be the event that a child has scoliosis, and ${S}_{0},{S}_{1},{S}_{2}$ be the event that a child practice $0,$ $1,$ or $2$ sports, respectively.\n\nLet's break down the information given in the problem statement:\n\n- From the bullet points, we have the following information:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & P({S}_{0})=0.25 &  & P(C|{S}_{0})=0.03 \\\\  & P({S}_{1})=0.30 &  & P(C|{S}_{1})=0.025 \\\\  & P({S}_{2})=0.45 &  & P(C|{S}_{2})=0.033\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe disjoint union ${S}_{0}⊔{S}_{1}⊔{S}_{2}$ covers the entire sample space, and we have\n\n$$\nP({S}_{0})+P({S}_{1})+P({S}_{2})=0.25+0.30+0.45=1.\n$$\n\n- Since $52%$ of children in the entire sample are female, we have\n\n$$\nP(F)=0.52.\n$$\n\n- Since $2%$ of females have scoliosis, we have\n\n$$\nP(C|F)=0.02.\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that a randomly chosen child with scoliosis is female, which is represented as $P(F|C).$ \n\nUsing Bayes' theorem combined with the law of total probability, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(F|C) & =\\frac{P(C|F)P(F)}{P(C)} \\\\  & =\\frac{P(C|F)P(F)}{P(C|{S}_{0})P({S}_{0})+P(C|{S}_{1})P({S}_{1})+P(C|{S}_{2})P({S}_{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(0.02)(0.52)}{(0.03)(0.25)+(0.025)(0.3)+(0.033)(0.45)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.0104}{0.0299} \\\\  & ≈0.348,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."} :step/id #uuid "964218e8-bf1c-559e-ad24-e50227ec5968" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550118}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550109 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487364 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8c32fc9e-ce9a-535b-a8b3-06c486c6cd67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.610" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486898} {:answer/id #uuid "a059a8b3-f1ed-5de8-a334-40e4ba40f83d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.602" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486959} {:answer/id #uuid "a6776cbd-ebfc-53b8-ad96-0550911cd2ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.688" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486972} {:answer/id #uuid "ec76cd0f-4509-5d07-9c06-06e62501a6f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.672" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487097} {:answer/id #uuid "efaec769-99d0-583b-aad4-773115f51b8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.668" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487107}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486898} :answer-field/id #uuid "55e23228-7c0d-5766-b1ab-6e8ce201deb8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487178}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "a806821e-50df-52c7-a557-34976fdac21e" :question/math-academy-id "q-148079" :question/problem "An English teacher believes that students who prefer cursive handwriting tend to perform better in essay writing. To prove his belief, he puts some students into groups of three according to their average writing grades and asks them if they usually write in cursive.\n\n- $20%$ of students have low grades, and $12%$ of those students usually write in cursive.\n\n- $65%$ of students have average grades, and $15%$ of those students usually write in cursive.\n\n- $15%$ of students have high grades, and $17%$ of those students usually write in cursive.\n\nIf $56%$ of students are female, and $16%$ of female students write in cursive, what is the probability that a randomly chosen student who writes in cursive is a female? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Let $F$ be the event that a student is female, $W$ be the event that the student writes in cursive, and $L,A,H$ be the events that the student has low, average, or high grades, respectively.\n\nLet's break down the information given in the problem statement:\n\n- From the bullet points, we have the following information:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & P(L)=0.2 &  & P(W|L)=0.12 \\\\  & P(A)=0.65 &  & P(W|A)=0.15 \\\\  & P(H)=0.15 &  & P(W|H)=0.17\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe disjoint union $L⊔A⊔H$ covers the entire sample space, and we have\n\n$$\nP(L)+P(A)+P(H)=0.2+0.65+0.15=1.\n$$\n\n- Since $56%$ of students are female, we have\n\n$$\nP(F)=0.56.\n$$\n\n- Since $16%$ of female students write in cursive, we have\n\n$$\nP(W|F)=0.16.\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that a randomly chosen student who writes in cursive is a female, which is represented as $P(F|W).$ \n\nUsing Bayes' theorem combined with the law of total probability, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(F|W) & =\\frac{P(W|F)P(F)}{P(W)} \\\\  & =\\frac{P(W|F)P(F)}{P(W|L)P(L)+P(W|A)P(A)+P(W|H)P(H)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(0.16)(0.56)}{(0.2)(0.12)+(0.65)(0.15)+(0.15)(0.17)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.0896}{0.147} \\\\  & ≈0.610,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."} :step/id #uuid "da95ad9e-2b99-5840-9da1-16c4078a085a" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550081}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550118 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487402 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10ee0324-47ce-520c-be1b-617fc28bde99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.004" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486628} {:answer/id #uuid "1f964113-d9c4-53ce-a730-891983a25308" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.06" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486654} {:answer/id #uuid "4dd21a5c-17af-557a-936c-d9cb120f8e34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.066" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486762} {:answer/id #uuid "56ac6ed1-d21c-50b6-90e0-44a21cfc1766" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.04" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486783} {:answer/id #uuid "8146efe8-c4c6-5ac7-a7c5-ddfd418eda3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.045" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486876}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486762} :answer-field/id #uuid "c75bc298-c38f-59f1-b1e6-e063f2062f22" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487249}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "f697c855-b9dd-5b7e-bb24-b23154c897b1" :question/math-academy-id "q-148085" :question/problem "A gambling room has three roulette tables, table $1,$ table $2,$ and table $3.$ Data of all bets of a particular evening are collected.\n\n- $30%$ of bets were played at table $1,$ and $5%$ of those bets were won.\n\n- $35%$ of bets were played at table $2,$ and $6%$ of those bets were won.\n\n- $35%$ of bets were played at table $3,$ and $7%$ of those bets were won.\n\nIf $10%$ of bets were played in the first hour, and $4%$ of bets made in the first hour were won, what is the probability that a randomly chosen winning bet was played in the first hour? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Let $F$ be the event that a bet is played in the first hour, $W$ be the event that a bet is won, and ${T}_{1},{T}_{2},{T}_{3}$ be the events that a bet is played at table $1,$ $2,$ or $3,$ respectively.\n\nLet's break down the information given in the problem statement:\n\n- From the bullet points, we have the following information:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & P({T}_{1})=0.3 &  & P(W|{T}_{1})=0.05 \\\\  & P({T}_{2})=0.35 &  & P(W|{T}_{2})=0.06 \\\\  & P({T}_{3})=0.35 &  & P(W|{T}_{3})=0.07\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe disjoint union ${T}_{1}⊔{T}_{2}⊔{T}_{3}$ covers the entire sample space, and we have\n\n$$\nP({T}_{1})+P({T}_{2})+P({T}_{3})=0.3+0.35+0.35=1.\n$$\n\n- Since $10%$ of bets in the entire evening are played in the first hour, we have\n\n$$\nP(F)=0.1.\n$$\n\n- Since $4%$ of bets made in the first hour are won, we have\n\n$$\nP(W|F)=0.04.\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that a randomly chosen winning bet is played in the first hour, which is represented as $P(F|W).$\n\nUsing Bayes' theorem combined with the law of total probability, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(F|W) & =\\frac{P(W|F)P(F)}{P(W)} \\\\  & =\\frac{P(W|F)P(F)}{P(W|{T}_{1})P({T}_{1})+P(W|{T}_{2})P({T}_{2})+P(W|{T}_{3})P({T}_{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(0.04)(0.1)}{(0.05)(0.3)+(0.06)(0.35)+(0.07)(0.35)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.004}{0.0605} \\\\  & ≈0.066,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."} :step/id #uuid "fce198a3-2fe3-5172-8c78-1731bf8adc70" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550043}}] :activity/title "Extending Bayes' Theorem" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549303}]}
